## Initial Q&A
### Did the Republican Party hold a majority in the Wisconsin State Assembly during the period from April 2020 to October 2020?
Answer: Yes, the Republican Party held a majority in the Wisconsin State Assembly during the period from April 2020 to October 2020. According to Ballotpedia, "Republicans held their majority in the 2018 elections for Wisconsin State Assembly, winning 63 seats to Democrats' 36." Since Wisconsin state Assembly members serve two-year terms with all seats up for election every two years, this majority would have continued until the next election in November 2020, meaning Republicans controlled the Assembly with a 63-36 majority throughout the April–October 2020 period.

Source URL: https://ballotpedia.org/Wisconsin_State_Assembly_elections,_2018

### Did the Republican Party hold a majority in the Wisconsin State Senate during the period from April 2020 to October 2020?
Answer: Yes, the Republican Party held a majority in the Wisconsin State Senate during the period from April 2020 to October 2020. The search result notes that after the 2018 elections, Republicans increased their majority in the Wisconsin State Senate from 18-15 to 19-14, and since state Senate terms are four years, this majority would have continued through the requested period without any intervening elections.

Source URL: https://ballotpedia.org/Wisconsin_State_Assembly_elections,_2018

