### Did Governor Tony Evers sign any bills into law between April 4, 2020, and October 4, 2020?
Answer: Yes, Governor Tony Evers signed at least one bill into law during that period. Specifically, he signed Assembly Bill 1038 (the COVID-19 Response Bill) on April 14, 2020, which became 2019 Wisconsin Act 185.

Source URL: https://lindner-marsack.com/news/2020/04

## Final Judgement
Based on the recorded evidence, the fact-check found that the Republican Party held majorities in both chambers of the Wisconsin State Legislature throughout the April–October 2020 period. Crucially, the evidence explicitly documents that the legislature passed at least one bill during this timeframe: Assembly Bill 1038 (the COVID-19 Response Bill) was passed by the Assembly on April 14, 2020, passed by the Senate on April 15, and signed into law by the Governor as 2019 Wisconsin Act 185.

The evidence is clear and directly contradicts the claim. Therefore, no further information is needed.

The claim asserts that "Wisconsin Republicans have not passed a single bill in 6 months" during the specified period. The recorded evidence shows that Republican-controlled chambers passed a bill into law in April 2020, which is within that six-month window. This explicitly refutes the claim. Therefore, the conclusion is `refuted`.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
Based on the fact-check, the verdict is **refuted** because the Republican Party held majorities in both chambers of the Wisconsin Legislature during the period in question, and evidence shows they passed at least one bill into law. Specifically, [Assembly Bill 1038](https://lindner-marsack.com/news/2020/04) (the COVID-19 Response Bill) was passed by the Republican-controlled Assembly on April 14, 2020, and by the Senate on April 15, 2020, directly contradicting the claim that no bills were passed.